Vacancies and vacancy clusters in bcc
=====================================

This example builds the starting configurations of a single vacancy and of
clusters of 2 to 10 vacancies in a bcc crystal.


Where it was used
-----------------

These are the vacancy clusters V_n, n = 1 to 10, of Figure 2 of

    A. M. Goryaeva, J. Dérès, C. Lapointe, P. Grigorev, T. D. Swinburne,
    J. R. Kermode, L. Ventelon, J. Baima and M.-C. Marinica,
    "Efficient and transferable machine learning potentials for the
    simulation of crystal defects in bcc Fe and W",
    Phys. Rev. Materials 5, 103803 (2021).
    https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevMaterials.5.103803

For bcc tungsten, that figure compares the formation energies of the clusters
given by machine-learning potentials (LML, QNML, GAP14, GAP19) with the DFT
values (panel a), and shows their binding energies as a function of the
cluster size (panel b). The clusters are similar to the configurations of

    D. R. Mason, D. Nguyen-Manh and C. S. Becquart,
    J. Phys.: Condens. Matter 29, 505501 (2017).

To reproduce the figure with a given potential:

    1. build the configurations with make_vacancies.sh (next section);
    2. bring bulk.lmp and every NNvM.lmp to the lattice parameter of
       tungsten for that potential (doc/concepts.md);
    3. relax each of them and take its energy;
    4. the formation energy of a cluster of n vacancies, in a cell of N
       sites, is  E(NNvM) - (N-n)/N x E(bulk);
    5. the binding energy compares it with n times the formation energy of
       the single vacancy, 01v1. It is positive for a bound cluster and
       negative for one that tends to dissociate.


How to run it
-------------

    sh make_vacancies.sh                 all configurations, 6x6x6 cubic cells
    sh make_vacancies.sh 8               all configurations, 8x8x8 cubic cells
    sh make_vacancies.sh 8 02v1 03v4     only the configurations named

The programs must be built first (doc/install.md). The results go to a
directory named after the cell, for instance 06x06x06:

    bulk.lmp  bulk.gin     the perfect crystal
    NNvM.lmp  NNvM.gin     the crystal with the vacancies of configuration NNvM

.lmp is a LAMMPS data file and .gin holds reduced coordinates (doc/files.md).
The crystal has the lattice parameter of the programs, 2.8553 A. To bring it
to the lattice parameter of your metal, see doc/concepts.md.

clean.sh removes the result directories.


The configurations
------------------

INPS_VAC/no00.inp_NNvM lists the vacant sites of configuration NNvM. NN is the
number of vacancies and M numbers the configurations of that size. The first
line of a file is the number of sites; the following lines are their
coordinates, in units of half the lattice parameter.

    01v1             the single vacancy
    02v1 ... 02v5    di-vacancies on first to fifth neighbours
    03v1 ... 10v2    clusters of 3 to 10 vacancies

All sites lie between 1 and 6, so the smallest cell is 4x4x4.

To add a configuration, add a file with the same layout to INPS_VAC.


data_vac.dat
------------

One line per configuration of two vacancies or more:

    column 1         name of the configuration, without the leading zero
    column 2         number of orientations of the cluster in the crystal
    column 3         mean squared distance between its vacancies, in units
                     of the squared first-neighbour distance
    columns 4 to 6   values of the study the configurations come from; the
                     programs do not use them

Columns 2 and 3 can be recomputed from the site lists. They agree for every
configuration except three, whose site lists give other values:

                  site list          data_vac.dat
    4v4           48   1.778         24   2.000
    10v1          24   1.956         48   2.037
    10v2          48   2.222         48   2.096
